# Account-Admin Service Principal credentials for integration-test environment provisioning.
# Used by:  python scripts/provision_test_env.py provision
#
# The Service Principal must have the Databricks Account Admin role.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABRICKS_ACCOUNT_ID
#   Your Databricks account UUID.
#   Account Console (https://accounts.cloud.databricks.com) > top-right menu > "Account ID"
#
# DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID / DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET
#   The SP's OAuth credentials.
#   Account Console > User Management > Service Principals > <your SP>
#   > "Application ID"  →  CLIENT_ID
#   > "OAuth Secrets"  > Generate Secret  →  CLIENT_SECRET
#
# DATABRICKS_AWS_REGION
#   AWS region for the new test workspace and metastore.
#
# S3 storage
#   The provision script automatically creates (and on teardown deletes) an S3
#   bucket named  genie-uc-test-<aws-account-id>.  Each test run gets its own
#   subfolder:  s3://genie-uc-test-<aws-account-id>/genie-test-<run-id>/
#   No bucket configuration is required.  Your AWS credentials must include:
#     s3:CreateBucket, s3:DeleteBucket, s3:PutPublicAccessBlock,
#     s3:ListBucketVersions, s3:DeleteObject, s3:DeleteObjectVersion
# S3 bucket is auto-created by the provision script as genie-uc-test-<aws-account-id>.
# Each test run creates a unique subfolder: s3://<bucket>/genie-test-<run-id>/
# The IAM role is created and scoped to this bucket automatically.
#
# ── AWS credentials (for automated IAM role management) ──────────────────────
#
# The provision script creates a fresh IAM role for each test run (and deletes
# it on teardown).  It needs AWS credentials with these IAM permissions:
#   iam:CreateRole, iam:DeleteRole, iam:PutRolePolicy, iam:DeleteRolePolicy,
#   iam:ListRolePolicies, iam:ListAttachedRolePolicies, iam:DetachRolePolicy,
#   iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy, sts:GetCallerIdentity
#
# ⚠  IMPORTANT: Use LONG-LIVED credentials (IAM user keys) for Option A/B/C.
#    Temporary STS tokens (AWS_SESSION_TOKEN) expire after 1–12 hours.  The
#    full test pipeline (provision + ~90 min tests + teardown) can outlast a
#    short-lived token, causing the IAM role to not be deleted on teardown.
#    If you must use STS tokens, request a session of at least 4 hours and
#    keep AWS_SESSION_TOKEN in the file; re-run teardown with fresh tokens if
#    you see "ExpiredToken" errors.
#
# Option A — explicit long-lived IAM user keys in this file (recommended for CI):
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID     = AKIAxxx          # starts with AKIA (permanent)
#   AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY = xxx
#   # Leave AWS_SESSION_TOKEN blank
#
# Option B — AWS named profile (leave the key fields blank):
#   AWS_PROFILE = my-admin-profile
#
# Option C — environment variables (export before running provision):
#   export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=AKIAxxx
#   export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=xxx
#   # Teardown re-reads os.environ, so exporting fresh tokens also works for retry
#
# Option D — instance role / ECS task role / SSO (leave all blank, boto3
#   picks up credentials automatically from the metadata service or keychain).
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECURITY: This file contains secrets.  It is gitignored.  Never commit it.
#
# Copy and fill in:
#   cp scripts/account-admin.env.example scripts/account-admin.env

DATABRICKS_ACCOUNT_ID       = 
DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID        = 
DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET    = 
DATABRICKS_AWS_REGION       = ap-southeast-2

# AWS credentials — choose one option from the comment above.
# Leave blank if using Option B/C/D.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID     = 
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY = 
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN     = 
# AWS_PROFILE         = my-admin-profile
